WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.260 --> 00:00:09.259
So as previously mentioned, Unreal has its own implementation of the C++ 11, I think, Smart Pointer Library.

2
00:00:09.259 --> 00:00:12.259
It's worth just going into a little bit.

3
00:00:12.259 --> 00:00:20.260
So anytime you have non-new objects that you want to have the memory managed for you in some way,

4
00:00:20.260 --> 00:00:22.260
Smart Pointers are definitely something to consider.

5
00:00:22.260 --> 00:00:26.260
You're probably already familiar with Smart Pointers for more C++ if that's something you've used before,

6
00:00:26.260 --> 00:00:29.260
but just to kind of briefly go through them.

7
00:00:29.260 --> 00:00:34.259
We have T-Shared Pointer, which is essentially a reference counted pointer.

8
00:00:34.259 --> 00:00:38.259
So as this reference is copied around and passed around,

9
00:00:38.259 --> 00:00:42.259
we keep track of how many things have access to that pointer.

10
00:00:42.259 --> 00:00:46.259
As that number of references hits zero, then that memory is freed.

11
00:00:46.259 --> 00:00:50.259
So we don't have to worry, it's kind of shared ownership of this pointer.

12
00:00:50.259 --> 00:00:56.259
T-Unique Pointer is kind of the opposite of that, where we say one thing manages this memory,

13
00:00:56.259 --> 00:01:02.259
so as and when this thing stops referencing it, we're going to free that memory, just automatically.

14
00:01:02.259 --> 00:01:08.260
And T-Weak Pointer is when we say this pointer doesn't manage that memory at all.

15
00:01:08.260 --> 00:01:12.260
It is conscious that that memory might get cleaned up underneath it.

16
00:01:12.260 --> 00:01:15.260
It's really helpful for things like lambdas that might get executed,

17
00:01:15.260 --> 00:01:21.260
and you're aware that execution may occur after the objects it's referencing are already deleted for other reasons.

18
00:01:21.260 --> 00:01:23.260
So a Weak Pointer is a way you can say,

19
00:01:23.260 --> 00:01:30.260
I'm not sure if this object still exists, but if it does, I want to perform some functions on it or something like that.

20
00:01:30.260 --> 00:01:38.260
So those are the kind of standard C++ smart pointer library types that you would be aware of.

21
00:01:38.260 --> 00:01:40.260
And then we also have two additional ones.

22
00:01:40.260 --> 00:01:44.260
We have T-Shared Ref, which is the relationship from pointer to ref,

23
00:01:44.260 --> 00:01:48.260
where a ref is essentially a promise that this can never be null.

24
00:01:48.260 --> 00:01:54.390
The same thing applies for a T-Shared Ref, it's just in the shared pointer library.

25
00:01:54.390 --> 00:01:56.390
And last of all, we have T-Weak Object Pointer.

26
00:01:56.390 --> 00:02:01.390
Whereas the rest of these are intended for non-U-Object types,

27
00:02:01.390 --> 00:02:05.390
T-Weak Object Pointer provides the same functionality as T-Weak Pointer,

28
00:02:05.390 --> 00:02:09.389
but specifically for U-Object types.

29
00:02:09.389 --> 00:02:13.389
So that same concept, again, the first thing I think of is lambdas.

30
00:02:13.389 --> 00:02:16.389
Whenever you're passing a reference to a U-Object,

31
00:02:16.389 --> 00:02:20.389
that you're aware that U-Object might be gone by the time that lambda executes,

32
00:02:20.389 --> 00:02:24.389
T-Weak Object Pointer is a thing to consider using.

33
00:02:24.389 --> 00:02:29.939
So the benefits of smart pointers, largely the same as C++ smart pointer benefits.

34
00:02:29.939 --> 00:02:32.939
So if you're not aware of them, it helps prevent memory leaks.

35
00:02:32.939 --> 00:02:36.939
That's a really, really common one. That's why they were introduced in the first place.

36
00:02:36.939 --> 00:02:42.939
Unlike U-Objects, where it's highly recommended, you don't access them off the game thread.

37
00:02:43.939 --> 00:02:47.939
The smart pointer library, particularly shared pointers, can be thread-safe.

38
00:02:47.939 --> 00:02:50.939
It's worth a highlight that there are two options when you create a shared pointer.

39
00:02:50.939 --> 00:02:55.939
It can be a thread-safe shared pointer, or it can be non-thread-safe.

40
00:02:55.939 --> 00:03:02.939
The thread-safe pointer has its reference counting done by an atomic operation,

41
00:03:02.939 --> 00:03:05.939
which essentially is an operation that occurs between threads.

42
00:03:05.939 --> 00:03:08.939
So there's no way that two threads can end up in a race condition

43
00:03:08.939 --> 00:03:10.939
for how many things are referencing this object.

44
00:03:10.939 --> 00:03:15.939
That's beneficial for thread safety, but it does come at a certain performance cost,

45
00:03:15.939 --> 00:03:17.939
so it's just worth bearing that in mind.

46
00:03:17.939 --> 00:03:20.939
And then the last thing is obviously the runtime safety of T-SharedRef,

47
00:03:20.939 --> 00:03:22.939
which is very, very useful.

48
00:03:22.939 --> 00:03:25.939
The other thing to point out is obviously this is an implementation

49
00:03:25.939 --> 00:03:28.939
of the C++ standard smart pointer library,

50
00:03:28.939 --> 00:03:33.939
but the reason we would recommend using Unreal's implementation of these things

51
00:03:33.939 --> 00:03:35.969
is it's platform agnostic.

52
00:03:35.969 --> 00:03:39.969
So when it comes to actually something that implements or makes use of this shared pointer library,

53
00:03:39.969 --> 00:03:43.969
it's worth highlighting that if it's from a non-reflected type,

54
00:03:43.969 --> 00:03:46.969
we need to inherit from the T-SharedThis class.

55
00:03:46.969 --> 00:03:48.969
So this is what an example implementation of that would look like.

56
00:03:48.969 --> 00:03:51.969
We have some class, then we inherit from T-SharedThis,

57
00:03:51.969 --> 00:03:53.969
and this is a generic implementation,

58
00:03:53.969 --> 00:03:56.969
so we need to provide the class that we're implementing

59
00:03:56.969 --> 00:04:00.969
as the argument there for the generic function.

60
00:04:00.969 --> 00:04:05.349
And what this does is it gives us access to a suite of functions.

61
00:04:05.349 --> 00:04:07.349
We have makeShared and makeSharable,

62
00:04:07.349 --> 00:04:11.349
which are basically two ways of creating a shared pointer,

63
00:04:11.349 --> 00:04:14.349
a shared reference for this type.

64
00:04:14.349 --> 00:04:17.350
makeShared is encouraged to be used over makeSharable

65
00:04:17.350 --> 00:04:19.540
because it's slightly more performant.

66
00:04:19.540 --> 00:04:25.540
And then asShared and sharedThis basically just give us a sharedRef to that type,

67
00:04:25.540 --> 00:04:28.540
but it's expecting that that type is already created.

68
00:04:28.540 --> 00:04:31.540
And then these are all about getting access to a sharedRef,

69
00:04:31.540 --> 00:04:33.540
and once we have that sharedRef,

70
00:04:33.540 --> 00:04:37.540
we can easily dereference that into a T-Shared pointer to then pass around

71
00:04:37.540 --> 00:04:42.540
if you don't want that runtime null checking safety.
